[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `SIGPIPE` disposition: C is killed by the signal, Rust exited 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdout connected to a pipe whose read end had already been
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m closed, the two programs disagreed on how they terminated:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | exit code | signal |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | *(none)* | 13 (`SIGPIPE`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before fix) | 0 | *(none)* |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproduced by `process_io::reader_hangup_on_stdout`, which spawns the program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with a piped stdout, drops the read end, then writes `1 2 3\n` to its stdin.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` during
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runtime setup, before `main` is entered. With the signal ignored, the failing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `write` returns `EPIPE`, and because the translation deliberately discards write
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m errors (matching C's habit of ignoring `printf` return values) the process ran
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to completion and exited 0. The C program never touches the disposition, so it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m keeps the default action and is killed by the signal instead. This is purely a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runtime-environment difference; no logic in the translation was wrong.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note that this was invisible to every stdout/stderr comparison — the signalled C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m process produces no output at all, and so did the Rust process, since its single
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m buffered write also failed. Only the exit status distinguished them, which is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exactly the failure mode the task warns about.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/main.rs` now resets the disposition to `SIG_DFL` as the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first statement of `main`, via a locally declared `signal` binding so no new
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m crate dependency is introduced:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m #[cfg(unix)]
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fn restore_default_sigpipe() {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     const SIGPIPE: i32 = 13;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     const SIG_DFL: usize = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     extern "C" { fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     unsafe { signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL); }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both programs now die with signal 13 and no exit code in this scenario.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours checked and confirmed already correct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
